\f1\fs22 No one had arrived before Sarah, not even Dr Quark, who frequently showed up to meetings thirty minutes early so he could get a few things done without interruption. She made a cup of tea and sat in the centre of the broadside of the table, from where she could gaze out at the rainforest. It was also the nearest seat to both the refreshments on the side board and the short hall to the bathroom.\
The second to arrive was a wallaby, who quietly loped past on two pairs of legs, apparently on some errand. Dr Quark arrived with Johnny attached, and looked flustered by being beset before the meeting had even started. Chip lumbered in and made the room feel like a cave.\
Kleo led the Sages in. Sarah noted they\'92d swapped their parrot robes of the last meeting for colours that she decided to call Corporate Camouflage. She wondered what they were up to.\
The Sages bid their greetings as they took the positions at the end of the table opposite Johnny, as if they might want to make a quick getaway.\
\'93You must be congratulated on the resounding success of the last test, young Sarah.\'94 Kleo smiled.\
Dr Quark looked over his glasses. He was used to a face being praised while the brains remained uncredited. \'93Shall we begin?\'94\
Johnny mouthed a few syllables that everyone instantly forgot, then Chip cut him off.\
Chip took control of the conference room screens and flicked up an image.\
Sarah had seen images like that before. It was a cross section cut through the Earth. On the curved surface were two points marked 
\f2\i Sublime Point
\f1\i0  and 
\f2\i Gibson Desert
\f1\i0 . Underneath a curved band of what Sarah assumed to represent the Earth\'92s crust, there were a geometry of lines drawn, both straight and wavy, representing angles and energy. There were Greek symbols and measured angles.\
\'93At the exact moment the Missing Sock message was received, there was a tremor in the Gibson Desert measuring 4.2\'97\'94\
Johnny interrupted. \'93I swear if you are going to mention Rutherford again\'97\'94\
\'93It was Rotherhithe,\'94 Chip grumbled.\
\'93See?\'94 Johnny lauded. \'93You\'92re a one-trick broken refrigerator repairman.\'94\
\'93Stasis technology technician,\'94 Chip corrected. \'93Who is bringing his expertise to the evidence\'97\'94\
\'93What? That spinning kebabs cause seismic activity over\'97what?\'97more than two and a half thousand kilometres away?\'94\
\'93That is my role here, to remind you of the risks and bring related geometries to your attention.\'94\
\'93Related geometries? What next? Are you going to give us all slide rules and teach us how to calculate the distance you can hit a golf ball?\'94\
\'93I can\'92t hit a golfball,\'94 Chip admitted gruffly.\
Sarah intervened. \'93That\'92s enough, Johnny. Mr Stockwell will be treated with respect.\'94\
\'93Oh, Sarah, if you understood the science\'97\'94\
\'93Mr Stockwell understands the science,\'94 Sarah said quietly.\
Chip shuffled uncomfortably on his chair, perhaps fearing he might break it.\
\'93Dr Quark?\'94 Sarah looked down the far end of the table.\
\'93Can you flick me the data?\'94\
Chip obliged. \
Dr Quark scrolled through the material on his device and measured his words. \'93I see a correlation. I see evidence. I don\'92t see a causation.\'94\
\'93There,\'94 Johnny said. \'93Thank you.\'94\
\'93That wasn\'92t support,\'94 Quark replied.\
Chilo straightened his robes.\
\'93I have more of the same. I have confirmation,\'94 Chip said, trying to maintain patience.\
\'93Then please share them with us. That\'92s how science works.\'94\
\'93What you\'92re doing is risky engineering.\'94\
\'93That\'92s how engineering works,\'94 Johnny corrected and thought he\'92d been smart.\
\'93Until it meets the Food Preservation Secrets Act,\'94 Chip said.\
\'93Ah,\'94 Dr Quark said. \'93That would be politics.\'94\
\'93That would be national security.\'94\
\'93How convenient.\'94\
Sarah drummed her fingers. \'93Clearly we cannot escalate the tests if we run the danger of causing earthquakes.\'94 Her head spun for a moment as she remembered Chanta\'92s can being thrown on its side, simply because she tossed a Gyges disk like a coin.\
\'93Clearly we should not put a mattress on legs if we run the danger of falling out of bed,\'94 Johnny said. He pointed at Chip. \'93Every time something happens that you don\'92t understand, he declares the end of civilisation.\'94\
There was an awkward silence. Chip pushed both hands flat on the table. \'93The likelihood of an earthquake in the Ulyssian desert happening concurrently with the state of the washing machine at that point is vanishingly low.\'94\
Johnny looked at Chip and laughed once. \'93You\'92ve wanted to sabotage my work since from the beginning. I\'92ll take this to Eloise,\'94 Johnny said. \'93There are too many misaligned resources on this project.\'94\
\'93No. I will take this to Eloise.\'94 Sarah considered and nodded. \'93I think you\'92re right about misaligned resources.\'94\
Johnny brightened. \'93Of course I am. This emaciated old bearskin should have been fired months ago.\'94\
Sarah wanted to defend Chip, but to say any more risked Chip\'92s betrayal of Fridge Force, of him leaking information to her that she should not, by law, possess. \'93Actually, Johnny, why are 
\f2\i you
\f1\i0  still here?\'94\
Johnny blinked.\
\'93Apart from acquiring kebabs\'97you seem remarkably good at conjuring Gyges discs, just at all the right moments, usually to save your skin.\'94\
Johnny was always fearless in the face of danger, mostly because he was too confident in himself to recognise it. \'93Well, now we can 
\f2\i all
\f1\i0  get kebabs from an Elvi truck parked at a funeral home. But who else here can supply the garlic sauce?\'94 Johnny tried to keep the smile off his face, and instead had to contend with choking down his laughter.\
\'93Tax!\'94 Sarah cursed. \'93I 
\f2\i hate
\f1\i0  garlic sauce.\'94\
Johnny\'92s smile melted off his face. \'93Chilli sauce, then.\'94\
\'93It\'92s not about the taxing condiments, Johnny! It\'92s about shaking the ground beneath your feet by tossing a bit of old boot leather.\'94\
\'93Sarah, I recognise the risks of unstable kebabs, I do. But we\'92ve stabilised them.\'94\
Little Clinton kicked low. Sarah stopped.\
Pain speared through her cervix and down between her legs. Her hand hit the table.\
\'93Sarah?\'94 Kleo said.\
She tried to breathe. Her belly went suddenly hard, then another sharp pain folded her over it.\
Chip was already moving.\
Johnny came around the table and squatted beside her chair. \'93Sarah, try to relax.\'94\
He reached for her hand. She flicked him away.\
\'93Don\'92t\'97\'94 Sarah forced her breathing to even out. She accepted the water Johnny offered. \'93Call Kathy.\'94\
Chip obliged. \'93She\'92ll be here right away.\'94\
Sarah nodded and relaxed.\
Johnny stood. He stood over Sarah.\
\'93Sarah, all this stress can\'92t be good for right now,\'94 Johnny placated. \'93You had me scared you were about to\'85 bleed out or something.\'94 He took a step back and almost feel into Chip\'92s lap. \'93You\'92re overdoing this.\'94 His voice had softened into something almost tender. \'93You need to\'85 rest. Perhaps you should take some time off. I mean, most other women would have been on maternity leave weeks ago.\'94\
Sarah looked up at him, imagined him crying to his mother with a torn fingernail. \'93I will take advice from a doctor\'97a female doctor. I will not take advice from a haircut on a sweaty neck like you.\'94\
\'93Good insult,\'94 Kleo muttered to himself and nodded.\
\'93Let us handle things here,\'94 Johnny soothed. \'93I mean, it\'92s not like you\'92re really in charge of anything here.\'94\
Even the Sages rolled their chairs back from the blast radius. Dr Quark used his fingers to test his face for hives.\
Sarah tried to straighten. Cramping took hold across her abdomen.\
\'93Look. Sarah,\'94 Johnny said. \'93You\'92re really not up to this.\'94\
Chip lifted Johnny off his feet, compacting Johnny\'92s lungs in his arms, and walked with him towards the bathrooms.\
\'93Stay in there!\'94 Chip ordered. \'93You\'92re about to piss yourself.\'94\
Chip was right.
